  title='About'
  description='Nicolas Borson is an Engineering Manager at Batch in Lyon, writing about how AI coding agents change code review, testing and software delivery.'
>
  <p>I’m Nicolas Borson, an Engineering Manager based in Lyon, France.</p>
  <p>
    I lead the Profile squad at <a href='https://batch.com' target='_blank'>Batch</a>, a B2B mobile
    marketing infrastructure company used by brands to reach and engage their users across app, web,
    email and SMS. My team owns the profiles platform, one of the most critical and heavily loaded
    parts of the product: it stores and maintains billions of user profiles and keeps them up to date
    as data flows in continuously from our customers’ apps, websites and backends.
  </p>
  <p>
    The challenges we work on are those of high-scale, real-time systems. Every event and attribute
    update has to be ingested, processed and reflected in a profile within seconds, so that marketers
    can segment, target and trigger messages based on what their users are doing right now. That
    means handling a constant, high-throughput stream of writes while serving low-latency reads, all
    with strong guarantees on data consistency and availability. Our stack is built around Kafka for
    streaming, TiDB as our distributed transactional store, and ClickHouse for analytics, along with
    the APIs and services that tie them together. Recent work has included migrating core profile
    storage to TiDB and defining SLOs on indexation lag, so that “real-time” remains a promise we can
    measure and hold ourselves to.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before becoming an Engineering Manager, I was a Technical Project Manager at Batch. I organized
    and tracked delivery across the engineering team, and led the change management effort to bring
    agile practices into how we worked day to day. That experience still shapes how I lead: I care
    about predictable delivery, clear priorities, and lightweight processes that help teams move
    faster.
  </p>
  <p>
    Alongside that, I have a strong infrastructure and DevOps background and like to stay close to
    the technical details: Kafka, Go, Docker and the operational realities of running systems in
    production. It helps me have meaningful conversations with my engineers, give them clear context
    and real ownership, and treat reliability and observability as first-class concerns so the
    platform can keep scaling without becoming harder to operate.
  </p>
  <p>
    I’ve been at Batch long enough to see it grow from a single engineering team into a squad-based
    organization, a transition I was directly involved in through my project management role.
    Helping teams stay effective through that kind of change, while keeping a demanding production
    system healthy, is one of the parts of the job I find most rewarding.
  </p>

